      <title>UK was ‘vehement’ slavery reparations should not be on Commonwealth summit agenda</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/01/uk-was-vehement-slavery-reparations-should-not-be-on-commonwealth-summit-agenda</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;British prime minister has now agreed to structured dialogue on reparatory justice but issue remains off formal Chogm agenda &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;British officials reportedly insisted that discussions of reparations for slavery should not be on the agenda of the Commonwealth leaders’ summit in Antigua and Barbuda – and even warned that Andy Burnham would not attend the meeting if the subject was formally included, according to a leading Caribbean political figure.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Ralph Gonsalves, a former prime minister of St Vincent and the Grenadines and a senior figure in the reparatory justice movement Repair Campaign, welcomed Britain’s decision to enter structured dialogue on reparations, but said the UK had come “kicking and screaming” to the conversation.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/01/uk-was-vehement-slavery-reparations-should-not-be-on-commonwealth-summit-agenda"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
